WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.066
Shell interpretation is a major source of process execution risk because a
shell treats text as instructions, not just as data.

2
00:00:07.066 --> 00:00:20.670
Shell features such as pipelines, expansion, redirection, pattern
matching, variables, quoting, and control syntax are useful for
interactive administration, but they can become dangerous when application
data is mixed into command text.

3
00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:27.471
The safest default is to avoid shell-mediated execution unless the feature
is truly required and carefully constrained.

4
00:00:27.471 --> 00:00:31.500
Direct process execution separates the executable from its arguments.

5
00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:37.509
In that model, the application identifies the program to run and passes
each argument as a structured value.

6
00:00:37.509 --> 00:00:41.801
This makes it harder for an ordinary input value to become command
structure.

7
00:00:41.801 --> 00:00:47.150
It also makes review clearer because the code shows which program runs and
which values are data.

8
00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:53.292
String-built command lines do the opposite: they blur the line between
command syntax and application input.

9
00:00:53.292 --> 00:00:57.584
Many shell conveniences have safer alternatives in Python or platform
APIs.

10
00:00:57.584 --> 00:01:04.056
Instead of relying on shell pipelines, the application may connect library
calls or process streams deliberately.

11
00:01:04.056 --> 00:01:09.141
Instead of relying on globbing, it may enumerate files with path APIs and
apply allowlists.

12
00:01:09.141 --> 00:01:14.622
Instead of using variable expansion or redirection, it may pass explicit
values and file handles.

13
00:01:14.622 --> 00:01:20.961
The exact implementation can vary, but the principle is stable: use
structured APIs when data might be untrusted.

14
00:01:20.961 --> 00:01:25.848
When shell behavior is unavoidable, the execution path should receive
extra scrutiny.

15
00:01:25.848 --> 00:01:36.480
The operation should be narrowly scoped, inputs should be mapped to
approved choices, the shell environment should be minimized, and sensitive
values should not be placed into command text.

16
00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:43.414
Reviewers should ask why the shell is needed, what input can influence it,
what privileges it has, and how failure is handled.

17
00:01:43.414 --> 00:01:47.640
Shell convenience should never be accepted as a substitute for safe
design.
